usb: dwc2: cv1800: enable host-mode DMA on CV18XX/SG200X

Mainline's dwc2_set_cv1800_params() forces the Sophgo CV18XX/SG200X DWC2
core into PIO (slave) mode for host transfers by setting host_dma = false.
In PIO mode every USB packet is copied by the CPU from inside the dwc2
interrupt handler. That is tolerable for enumeration and low-rate devices,
but a high-throughput USB device that keeps a bulk-IN stream open (e.g. an
MT7601U Wi-Fi adapter, which starts continuous RX as soon as it is
initialised) generates a relentless interrupt load. On this single-core
C906 that livelocks the CPU inside the IRQ handler: the console and
scheduler stop and the board appears frozen. The lockup reproduces right
after the mt7601u firmware handshake, before the adapter registers with
mac80211.

Enable buffer DMA for host mode instead. This is safe on this SoC:

  - The Sophgo vendor 5.10 kernel runs this same core with host DMA
    enabled (params.c: p->host_dma = dma_capable).
  - The SoC is dma-noncoherent, but the DMA API's cache maintenance is
    already exercised successfully by the stmmac Ethernet and SD/eMMC
    controllers on this board, which do bus-master DMA. dwc2 uses the
    same streaming-DMA path.

Only plain buffer DMA is enabled (host_dma = true); descriptor DMA
(dma_desc_enable) is deliberately left off, as buffer DMA is the
well-tested dwc2 host configuration. g_dma is left false because these
boards are only used in host mode here.

With this change the MT7601U initialises and the system stays responsive.

Signed-off-by: d622 <300083818+d622-au@users.noreply.github.com>

diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c b/drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c
index ccccccc..ddddddd 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c
@@ -224,7 +224,7 @@ static void dwc2_set_cv1800_params(struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg)

 	p->otg_caps.hnp_support = false;
 	p->otg_caps.srp_support = false;
-	p->host_dma = false;
+	p->host_dma = true;
 	p->g_dma = false;
 	p->speed = DWC2_SPEED_PARAM_HIGH;
 	p->phy_type = DWC2_PHY_TYPE_PARAM_UTMI;
